# --- VENDORED: bundled inside pip itself, not separately installable ---------
#
# A FOURTH CLASS, and the only one where "a fix exists" is true of the package
# and false of this image. Both are libraries pip VENDORS -- it ships its own
# copies under pip/_vendor/ and imports those, never site-packages -- so there
# is nothing to upgrade independently and no pip flag that swaps them out.
#
# Measured rather than inferred. pip is at 26.2.1, the current release, in both
# the venv and the base image's system python; its own manifest says:
#
#     /usr/local/lib/python3.11/site-packages/pip/_vendor/vendor.txt:15
#         setuptools==70.3.0
#     ...same file
#         msgpack==1.1.2
#
# Those are the versions Trivy reports, and they are NOT the setuptools and
# msgpack the project uses: the venv has setuptools 84.0.0 installed normally,
# which this build upgraded and which produces no finding. Upgrading pip is
# already done and does not move these; the remediation is upstream re-vendoring
# in a future pip, which is why nothing here can take it.
#
# THIS IS ALSO WHY THE EARLIER FIX LOOKED LIKE IT FAILED: upgrading setuptools
# in the venv and then in the system python each cleared real findings, and left
# these two, because they were never the copies being upgraded.
CVE-2025-47273         # HIGH  setuptools 70.3.0 vendored in pip  fixed 78.1.1
GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964    # HIGH  msgpack 1.1.2 vendored in pip      fixed 1.2.1

# --- BASE-LAG: Debian HAS fixed it; no published base image carries the fix ---
#
# A FIFTH CLASS, and the only TEMPORARY one in this file. Every entry above is
# blocked by a constraint that needs a DECISION to lift — a pin cap, a model
# risk, an upstream re-vendoring. These four are blocked only by the calendar,
# and they are not a surprise: they are the alarm the Dockerfile's two FROM
# lines are designed to raise. The gate is `--ignore-unfixed`, so it fires
# precisely when Debian ships a fix, and a pinned digest cannot receive one.
#
# THE CONSTRAINT, MEASURED 2026-08-20 — the fix exists and there is nowhere to
# take it from:
#
#     Debian fixed util-linux in 2.41.5-0+deb13u1  (DSA-6442-1, 2026-08-14).
#
#     pinned base    python:3.11-slim@sha256:94c50be2...  ->  util-linux 2.41-5
#     NEWEST PUBLISHED              @sha256:9c900dea...  ->  util-linux 2.41-5
#
# 9c900dea is what BOTH the `3.11-slim` and `3.11-slim-trixie` tags resolve to
# today; its image config is dated 2026-08-13, ONE DAY BEFORE the DSA. The two
# versions were read out of /var/lib/dpkg/status inside each image's layers on
# linux/amd64 — the arch CI scans — rather than inferred from a tag date. So
# re-pinning the digest, which is the one supported way in, has no target.
#
# THE OTHER WAY IN IS `apt-get upgrade`, AND IT STAYS REVERTED. Its removal is
# argued with a measurement at STAGE 2 of the Dockerfile: it cleared zero
# findings and it destroys the only reproducibility guarantee the image has.
# "It would clear something today" does not reopen that argument — a build that
# takes whatever Debian published this morning makes two builds of one commit
# differ, which is the property the digest pin exists to provide.
#
# REMOVAL CONDITION, mechanical rather than a judgement call. When Docker Hub
# publishes a python:3.11-slim whose util-linux is >= 2.41.5-0+deb13u1:
#     1. re-pin BOTH FROM lines to that digest, in one commit;
#     2. run `docker run --rm <image> python --version` and confirm 3.11.x,
#        which the Dockerfile demands after ANY digest change;
#     3. DELETE ALL FOUR LINES BELOW, and this block with them.
# They are accepted findings with an expiry, not suppressions. Nothing else in
# this file has a removal condition that can be checked by a script; this one
# can, and leaving it here after the digest moves is the failure mode.
#
# CVE-2026-53615 IS IN THE LIST AND IS NOT IN THE DSA. Checked per id rather
# than assumed from the advisory text: the Debian tracker records trixie as
# vulnerable at 2.41-5 and trixie-security as fixed at 2.41.5-0+deb13u1 — the
# same upload — so it clears with the same digest move as the other three.
#
# NOT LISTED HERE, DELIBERATELY: CVE-2026-13595 and CVE-2026-27456, the other
# two ids in DSA-6442-1. Both ARE present in this image and both clear with the
# same digest move, but Trivy 0.73.0 rates them MEDIUM, so the gate's
# `--severity HIGH,CRITICAL` never sees them. An entry would suppress nothing
# today while silently swallowing a future re-rating to HIGH — which is exactly
# the alarm this file must not disarm. The non-gating FULL scan prints them.
#
# THE FOUR IDS SPAN NINE BINARY PACKAGES built from the util-linux source —
# bsdutils, libblkid1, liblastlog2-2, libmount1, libsmartcols1, libuuid1,
# login, mount, util-linux — which is 36 gate rows from four ids. They are
# listed by id and not by package, per this file's rule: a NEW util-linux CVE
# must still fail the gate.
CVE-2026-53612   # HIGH   fixed 2.41.5-0+deb13u1   accepted 2026-08-20
CVE-2026-53613   # HIGH   fixed 2.41.5-0+deb13u1   accepted 2026-08-20
CVE-2026-53614   # HIGH   fixed 2.41.5-0+deb13u1   accepted 2026-08-20
CVE-2026-53615   # HIGH   fixed 2.41.5-0+deb13u1   accepted 2026-08-20
